# 10. File Hashes

A hash creates a digital fingerprint of a file.

For example:

```bash
sha256sum file.txt
```

Example:

```text
abc123...  file.txt
```

If the file changes, its SHA-256 hash normally changes.

---

# 11. Creating a File Integrity Baseline

Suppose we want to monitor:

```text
/etc/passwd
```

Create a baseline:

```bash
sha256sum /etc/passwd
```

Save the output:

```bash
sha256sum /etc/passwd > passwd.hash
```

View it:

```bash
cat passwd.hash
```

Later, calculate the hash again:

```bash
sha256sum /etc/passwd
```

Compare the result with the baseline.

---

# 12. Hash Comparison

Example baseline:

```text
HASH_A
```

Current hash:

```text
HASH_A
```

Result:

```text
No content change detected
```

If the current hash becomes:

```text
HASH_B
```

then:

```text
Hash changed
      ↓
File content may have changed
      ↓
Investigate
```

A hash change does not by itself prove malicious activity.

